     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-09 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>47-Million-Year-Old Owl Fossil Suggests Diurnal Origins</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A remarkably preserved 47-million-year-old owl fossil, Masillastrix blattophaga, discovered in Germany’s Messel Pit, indicates that early owls were diurnal. Researchers identified iridescent cockroach remains in the specimen's stomach, alongside wing and feather structures inconsistent with nocturnal activity. </p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-09/47-million-year-old-owl-fossil-suggests-diurnal-origins.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Senckenberg Institute scientists believe this discovery provides critical evidence that nocturnal behavior in owls evolved between 47 and 34 million years ago during the middle to late Eocene. The fossil, which is nearly complete, challenges previous assumptions about when owls transitioned to night-hunting, suggesting their specialized nocturnal adaptations developed significantly later in their evolutionary history.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately synthesizes the key scientific findings and context from the source without padding.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-night-owl-extraordinary-bird-fossil.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
