     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · BBC World · 2026-11-09 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Blast at Turkish Steel Plant Leaves 17 Dead</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#world</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>An explosion at the Habas Demir Celik iron and steel plant in Aliaga, Turkey, has resulted in 17 deaths and 12 injuries. The local governor reported that the accident occurred during scheduled maintenance when a crane carrying a replacement boiler collapsed, causing debris to fall into an electric arc furnace.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-09/blast-at-turkish-steel-plant-leaves-17-dead.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan confirmed investigations are underway, and authorities have arrested at least five people. While some eyewitnesses initially mistook the blast for an earthquake, search and rescue teams continue to operate at the site, which remains a key hub for Turkey's steel production and shipbreaking industry.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief effectively summarizes the industrial accident in Turkey and includes the corrected death toll from the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cq2d6d5z9y5jo?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
