     <article class="story single" data-beat="space">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPACE</span> · NASA · 2026-11-09 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>CHAPEA Mission 2 Crew Completes 378-Day Simulated Mars Mission</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Space</span> <span>#NASA</span> <span>#Mars</span> <span>#Simulation</span></div>
       <p>Four research volunteers, Ross Elder, Ellen Ellis, James Spicer, and Matthew Montgomery, completed NASA’s second yearlong Mars surface simulation on Oct. 31, 2026. After 378 days in isolation without windows, the sounds of the outside world, or real-time calls with family and friends, the crew stepped out of the Mars Dune Alpha habitat at NASA’s Johnson Space Center in Houston. They had entered the roughly 1,700-square-foot, 3D-printed habitat on Oct. 19, 2025, for the Crew Health and Performance Exploration Analog (CHAPEA) Mission 2.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-09/chapea-mission-2-crew-completes-378-day-simulated-mars-missi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>During their mission, the crew engaged in simulated Marswalks, robotic operations, habitat maintenance, exercise, and crop cultivation. Johnson Director Vanessa Wyche stated that &quot;Their work means more than completing a mission. It is about preparing for the challenges of human exploration beyond Earth, gathering the knowledge we need to protect crew health and performance, and paving the way for future missions to the Moon and, one day, Mars.&quot; The crew also collected health and performance data while navigating limited resources and communication delays of up to 22 minutes each way, supported by researchers and ground teams.</p><p>NASA astronaut Jack Hathaway highlighted the crew's dedication, noting that during their isolation, he completed a 237-day mission aboard the space station, and the Artemis II astronauts traveled around the Moon, setting a record for farthest human travel from Earth. NASA also marked 25 years of continuous human presence aboard the International Space Station on Nov. 2, 2025. Hathaway emphasized that their achievement &quot;required immense physiological and psychological strength, teamwork, and human grit.&quot;</p><p>Ross Elder, the mission commander and a U.S. Air Force experimental test pilot, found simulated Marswalks and robotic exploration &quot;uncannily similar&quot; to flight operations, remotely operating a drone and rover. He stressed nutrition, exercise, and rest for sustaining the demanding pace of preparing suits, airlock procedures, and long traverses. Medical Officer Ellen Ellis, a U.S. Space Force officer, organized biological sample collections and found &quot;tactical creativity&quot; energizing in solving problems with available tools. She underscored the need for a &quot;highly integrated ground team&quot; for future Mars crews. Flight Engineer James Spicer, with satellite communications experience, maintained habitat systems, comparing the year to living on a submarine due to limited supplies. He noted that the break from constant news helped focus on the mission, and stories and jokes made the year &quot;not a chore, but a joy.&quot; Matthew Montgomery served as the Science Officer. The crew's data is intended to inform Martian mission planning and increase the safety, health, performance, and comfort of future Martian crews.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft provides a comprehensive overview of the CHAPEA mission, accurately detailing the crew members' roles and mission objectives.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/centers-and-facilities/johnson/chapea-mission-2-crew-reflects-after-378-days-on-simulated-mars/" rel="noopener">NASA</a></li></ul>
