     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-11-09 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Congress Slams Modi Government Over Air Pollution Mortality Denial, Cites FICCI-EY Report</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Air Pollution</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#Congress</span> <span>#Modi Government</span></div>
       <p>The Congress party on Monday, November 9, 2026, criticized the Modi government for its perceived &quot;denial&quot; regarding the impact of air pollution on mortality rates in India. The party called for urgent measures, including tightening existing PM2.5 and ozone standards and revamping the National Clean Air Programme.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-09/congress-slams-modi-government-over-air-pollution-mortality-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Jairam Ramesh, Congress general secretary in-charge communications, cited a detailed FICCI-EY report. This report categorically states that approximately 2 million deaths in India in 2023, constituting about 22% of total fatalities, were attributable to air pollution. Ramesh asserted that the Modi government has consistently been in denial, having previously rejected numerous studies on the grounds they were not India-specific or that impacts were not directly attributable. He described the FICCI-EY report as &quot;yet another wakeup call for the somnolent Modi Government,&quot; emphasizing that &quot;ease of breathing is even more fundamental&quot; than ease of doing business, and noting that PM2.5 has become a &quot;decided killer&quot; across many parts of India.</p><p>The FICCI report, according to Ramesh, offers an &quot;essential and clearly phased ten-year roadmap for national action.&quot; He urged immediate execution, beginning with tightening existing PM2.5 and ozone standards to World Health Organization (WHO) recommended safety levels. Further recommendations included revamping the National Clean Air Programme to specifically target PM2.5 and ozone, and enacting a new Clean Air Act focused on public health, replacing the Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1981, given the extensive new evidence on pollution sources and health impacts. Ramesh stressed that the government could not dismiss the analysis from the country's leading apex industry body.</p><p>These remarks coincided with a thick grey haze blanketing Delhi, a day after Diwali, significantly reducing visibility. The city's Air Quality Index (AQI) reached 370, nearing the 'severe' category. Specific monitoring stations like DTU and Burari Crossing recorded an AQI of 437 each, indicating the worst air quality. While four other stations registered 'poor' air quality, the remaining fell into the 'very poor' category. The Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB) classifies an AQI between 401 and 500 as 'severe'. Several areas, including Bawana, Burari, Jahangangirpuri, Sonia Vihar, Punjabi Bagh, Okhla, and Rohini, experienced 'severe' air quality. The Air Quality Early Warning System predicts Delhi's air quality is likely to remain in the 'severe' category over the subsequent days. Last week, the Aam Aadmi Party (AAP) had accused the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) government of &quot;manipulating&quot; AQI data by spraying water on monitoring stations and alleged that &quot;fudging&quot; AQI data was &quot;life-threatening.&quot;</p><p>What to watch: Government response to the FICCI-EY report and Congress's calls for action amidst ongoing air quality challenges.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The report accurately details Ramesh's criticisms, the FICCI-EY report statistics, and the current Delhi air quality situation.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/congress-slams-modi-govt-for-being-in-denial-on-impact-of-air-pollution-on-mortality/article71681683.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
