     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · Times of India · 2026-11-09 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Harvard Team's Rumble Rims Wins Global James Dyson Medical Award</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Concussion</span> <span>#Headset</span> <span>#Medical Technology</span> <span>#James Dyson Award</span></div>
       <p>Radhika Saran, an Indian-origin Harvard University student, along with teammates Cleo Pontone and Sophia DeVito, has developed Rumble Rims, a low-cost concussion-screening headset. This invention recently won the global medical category of the 2026 James Dyson Award, recognized as one of the world's prominent design and engineering competitions.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-09/harvard-teams-rumble-rims-wins-global-james-dyson-medical-aw.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>On November 4, 2026, Sir James Dyson selected Rumble Rims as one of two global winners, with the team set to receive £30,000 in prize money for further development. The current prototype was built for under $350, aiming to make objective concussion screening accessible to schools, community sports programs, and grassroots-level teams, a significant improvement over comparable eye-tracking technologies that can cost $25,000 or more.</p><p>Detecting concussions in sports, particularly among young athletes in contact sports like football and rugby, poses a serious concern. Athletes may not show immediate signs of injury, with symptoms potentially taking up to 72 hours to develop. Decisions on whether an athlete can continue playing are often made on the sidelines with limited time and resources, and symptoms vary among individuals.</p><p>Rumble Rims utilizes advanced infrared camera technology to track an athlete's eye movements. This method draws on research indicating that neurological disruption following a concussion can affect how eyes move and follow objects. During a two to three-minute test, an athlete wears the headset and follows a moving target displayed on an iPad screen. The infrared cameras measure eye tracking, focus, and movement, translating the data into information on a mobile display for coaches and trainers. The device also includes a recovery dashboard for repeated testing over time.</p><p>The students initially explored integrating eye-tracking into helmets and mouthguards. They noted their surprise at the common reliance on simple methods like asking an athlete to follow a finger, which depend heavily on human judgment. The team's next goals include developing a more advanced prototype, enhancing the accuracy of the eye-movement test, and validating the technology with athletes, coaches, trainers, and medical advisers.</p><p>What to watch: Further development and validation of Rumble Rims for broader adoption in sports safety protocols.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft provides a comprehensive and accurate summary of the source material, including all relevant details about the invention and the team.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/life-style/people/an-india-raised-harvard-student-radhika-saran-and-two-teammates-built-a-concussion-screening-headset-for-under-350-months-after-winning-a-us-award-their-invention-has-now-won-a-global-james-dyson-prize-worth-40000/articleshow/135677301.cms" rel="noopener">Times of India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
