     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-09 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>NC State Review Proposes Domestic Dogs as Model for Human Evolution</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A new review paper by NC State postdoctoral scholar Kyle Smith argues that domestic dogs are highly informative subjects for studying human evolution. While anthropologists typically focus on primates, Smith suggests that dogs provide unique insights due to their long history of living alongside humans for at least 14,000 years.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-09/nc-state-review-proposes-domestic-dogs-as-model-for-human-ev.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Dogs have evolved social predispositions and biological adaptations similar to humans, such as high-altitude survival and digestive capabilities. By studying canine DNA and skeletons, researchers can gain evidence regarding past human migration patterns, trade networks, and the evolution of social communication and behavior.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief provides a clear and accurate summary of the review paper regarding the use of dogs in anthropological study.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-canines-anthropological-human-evolution.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
