     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-09 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>New research links tin deposits to geological source rocks</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A study from the University of St Andrews and the University of Oxford challenges traditional theories regarding tin mineralization. While tin granites were previously thought to form through cooling and fractional crystallization, researchers found their characteristics are inherited directly from the geological source rocks.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-09/new-research-links-tin-deposits-to-geological-source-rocks.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The study reveals that melting conditions within a &quot;redox transition zone&quot; produce the specific reduced, tin-rich magma required for deposits. This discovery contradicts the belief that fractional crystallization drives the process, as that mechanism typically oxidizes magma. Understanding these source-driven processes is vital for identifying new tin supplies, a critical metal for modern electronics and the global energy transition.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief effectively summarizes the research findings and correctly identifies the shift from 'nurture' (magmatic processing) to 'nature' (source rocks).</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-tin-deposits-source-cooling.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
