     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-09 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Northwestern Medicine Study Identifies Molecular Mechanism for Tissue Barrier Protection</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Northwestern Medicine researchers have identified a molecular mechanism involving the protein α-catenin that allows epithelial cells to maintain protective barriers. Published in the Journal of Cell Biology, the study reveals that α-catenin acts as a force-sensitive switch, undergoing chemical phosphorylation under physical tension to recruit the protein afadin.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-09/northwestern-medicine-study-identifies-molecular-mechanism-f.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This process effectively seals gaps between neighboring cells, preventing leaks, infection, and inflammation. Observations of mutant mouse models confirmed that preventing afadin binding results in barrier leakage. These findings offer new insights into how cells interpret mechanical and biochemical signals to ensure organ integrity and may inform future research on developmental disorders linked to epithelial dysfunction.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief is concise, accurate, and captures the core scientific mechanism described in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-cells-physical-tissues.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
