     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · News18 World · 2026-11-09 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Nvidia CEO Affair Linked to Short Seller Report</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#world</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Months before Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang admitted to an extramarital affair, short-selling firm Culper Research flagged his relationship with Chinese businesswoman Alice Huang as &quot;uncomfortably close.&quot; A May 2026 report detailed public sightings of the pair in Taiwan but did not confirm an affair or espionage.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-09/nvidia-ceo-affair-linked-to-short-seller-report.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Culper's report also questioned the financial activities of Singapore-based Megaspeed, where Alice previously held an executive role, noting a massive increase in balance sheet deposits. While the report lacked evidence of wrongdoing, subsequent media coverage alleged Alice was tasked with influencing Jensen regarding US export restrictions on China.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Provides a precise summary of the Culper Research report and the subsequent allegations mentioned in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.news18.com/world/uncomfortably-close-jensen-huang-alice-affair-was-flagged-by-us-short-seller-months-ago-ws-l-10418309.html" rel="noopener">News18 World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
