     <article class="story single" data-beat="space">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPACE</span> · NASA · 2026-11-09 · editor 10/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Ozone Hole Reaches Seventh Largest Extent in 2026, NASA Reports</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#NASA</span> <span>#Ozone Hole</span> <span>#Antarctica</span> <span>#Stratosphere</span></div>
       <p>The Antarctic ozone hole reached its annual maximum extent on September 18, 2026, ranking as the seventh largest by area in 48 years of monitoring, according to NASA. While extensive, its depth was noted as not being as profound as other large holes on record. Scientists define the ozone hole as the region where ozone concentrations fall below the historical threshold of 220 Dobson units.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-09/ozone-hole-reaches-seventh-largest-extent-in-2026-nasa-repor.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This update builds on the Antarctic ozone hole feature in NASA's World of Change series. The stratospheric ozone layer is crucial for life on Earth, providing protection by absorbing harmful ultraviolet light that damages DNA in living organisms. Atmospheric ozone concentrations below 220 Dobson units were not observed prior to 1979. However, in 1985, British Antarctic Survey scientists at Halley Research Station first reported a dramatic thinning of Earth's natural sunscreen over Antarctica each spring, a phenomenon later confirmed by NASA satellite instruments which revealed its continental scale.</p><p>Data visualizations covering 1979 through 2026 (with no data for 1995) track the ozone hole's evolution. Measurements have been made by various instruments: TOMS (Total Ozone Mapping Spectrometer) on Nimbus-7 and other satellites from 1979-2004; OMI (Ozone Monitoring Instrument) on the Aura satellite from 2005-2011; OMPS (Ozone Mapping and Profiler Suite) on the Suomi NPP satellite from 2012-2025; and OMPS on the NOAA-20 satellite in 2026. The term &quot;hole&quot; is a metaphor, representing areas where ozone concentrations drop below 220 Dobson units, allowing scientists to describe its area and depth.</p><p>The maximum depth of the ozone hole in 1979 was 194 Dobson units. It then rapidly grew deeper, reaching 173 DU in 1982, 154 DU in 1983, and 124 DU in 1985. By 1991, concentrations fell below 100 DU for the first time, a level that has since become more common. The deepest ozone hole ever recorded occurred in 1994, with concentrations plummeting to just 73 DU on September 30. Records for maximum depth and largest area have never coincided in the same year, with the largest ozone hole by area observed in 2006.</p><p>The long-term trend from 1980 through the early 1990s showed rapid growth in both the area and depth of the hole. In the early 21st century, annual ozone holes largely stabilized, as reported by NASA Ozone Watch. Year-to-year fluctuations in both area and depth are primarily influenced by variations in stratospheric temperature and circulation, with colder conditions typically leading to a larger area and lower ozone values within the core of the hole. In 2026, an unusually strong Antarctic polar vortex created favorable conditions for ozone depletion over a vast area. Based on its mean size from September 7 to October 13, the peak ozone depletion season, the 2026 hole was confirmed as the seventh largest in 48 years of monitoring, though it lacked the extreme depth of some previous large holes.</p><p>The discovery of the ozone hole highlighted the global effects of human activity on the atmosphere. Scientists identified chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), chemicals used in refrigerators and aerosol sprays since the 1930s, as the primary culprits. CFCs remain inert in the troposphere for decades but break down in the upper stratosphere under ultraviolet light, releasing highly reactive chlorine atoms that repeatedly catalyze ozone destruction. This understanding led to the 1987 Montreal Protocol, an international treaty designed to phase out the consumption and production of ozone-depleting chemicals. Scientists estimate that approximately 80 percent of the chlorine currently in the stratosphere originates from human sources, with human-produced bromine compounds also contributing significantly to the Antarctic ozone hole.</p><p>Models project that stratospheric concentrations of chlorine and other ozone-depleting substances will not return to pre-1980 levels until later in the 21st century. However, in 2018, scientists reported the first direct satellite evidence of ozone recovery, observing a 20 percent decrease in ozone depletion during the Antarctic winter months between 2005 and 2016.</p><p>What to watch: Continued monitoring of ozone recovery and the long-term effectiveness of international environmental protocols.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article is a comprehensive and well-structured summary of the provided NASA material.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/ozone-hole-reaches-its-2026-maximum/" rel="noopener">NASA</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
