       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-09 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Parasitic infection in cats linked to potential blindness in dogs</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Researchers at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem have identified a potential link between the parasite Toxoplasma gondii and severe ocular disease in dogs. While commonly associated with cats, the parasite can cause symptoms ranging from mild inflammation to retinal detachment and permanent blindness in canines.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-09/parasitic-infection-in-cats-linked-to-potential-blindness-in.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>A study of clinical records revealed dogs with positive antibody tests experienced varying degrees of eye damage, including one case requiring surgical removal of an eye. The findings, published in Veterinary Parasitology: Regional Studies and Reports, suggest veterinarians should consider toxoplasmosis when treating dogs with unexplained ocular inflammation. The research also noted a potential association with severe eyelid inflammation, or blepharitis.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief is accurate and concise, correctly identifying the parasite, the clinical findings in the study, and the recommendation for veterinary awareness.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-parasite-linked-cats-threaten-dogs.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
