     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · Times of India · 2026-11-09 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Supreme Court Affirms Government Authority for Compulsory Retirement</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Supreme Court of India has ruled that the government possesses broad powers to compulsorily retire employees to improve administrative efficiency. A bench of Justices Manmohan and Shree Chandrashekhar held that compulsory retirement is not punitive, meaning the constitutional requirement to hear an employee before punishment does not apply to such administrative decisions.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-09/supreme-court-affirms-government-authority-for-compulsory-re.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The court upheld the Chhattisgarh government's retirement of five employees, noting that the decision rests on the subjective satisfaction of the competent authority. Justices emphasized that courts should only review whether relevant material was considered by authorities and whether the conclusion was reasonable, rejecting challenges based on the treatment of other employees.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief correctly identifies the court's ruling, the legal reasoning regarding administrative efficiency, and the specific case details.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/sc-govt-has-wide-powers-to-compulsorily-retire-staffer-improve-efficiency/articleshow/135692813.cms" rel="noopener">Times of India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
