     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-09 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Taung Child Fossil Dated to 3 Million Years Ago</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A new study in npj Palaeoecosystems dates the 1924 Taung Child fossil to between 2.96 and 3.03 million years old. Lead researcher Gabriele Macho of Vrije Universiteit Brussel suggests the findings indicate the human family tree was a complex, tangled bush shaped by climate-driven evolutionary diversity rather than a linear progression.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-09/taung-child-fossil-dated-to-3-million-years-ago.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Analysis of tooth structure and brain development reveals that several adult fossils previously grouped with the Taung Child do not match its trajectory. The research suggests multiple distinct hominin species coexisted in the Sterkfontein region, as climate fluctuations forced populations to adapt, diverge, and hybridize within changing tropical and temperate ecosystems.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately synthesizes the new research on the Taung Child and its implications for human evolutionary models.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-million-year-taung-child-human.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
