     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · Good News Network · 2026-11-09 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Teen's Kora App Helps Hundreds of Families Interpret Autistic Cues</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Autism</span> <span>#Technology</span> <span>#Innovation</span> <span>#Youth Achievement</span></div>
       <p>A New Jersey high schooler, Shashank Madala, developed a free app called Kora to help parents and caregivers interpret the emotional cues of autistic children. His initiative stemmed from observing adults frequently misinterpreting the signals from his autistic cousin, who would often become upset at family gatherings. The adults' incorrect guesses led his cousin to stop trying to communicate his feelings, prompting Madala, then 15 with only one computer science class, to take action.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-09/teens-kora-app-helps-hundreds-of-families-interpret-autistic.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Madala taught himself machine learning to create Kora, which now supports over 700 users across 12 countries, reaching families primarily through autism therapy centers. The app is designed to present several possible readings of what a child might be feeling, such as happy, sad, angry, afraid, or neutral, along with suggested responses. Crucially, Kora avoids offering single, confident answers, scores, diagnoses, or judgments, a lesson Madala learned from his early versions which repeated the very mistake his family had made. He rebuilt the app to encourage adults to slow down and consider multiple possibilities.</p><p>The philosophy behind Madala's work is to shift the focus from changing the child to equipping adults with better tools for understanding. As he wrote in Autism Spectrum News in August, &quot;A tool that announces that a child is angry invites the adult to stop looking at the child.&quot; Demonstrating responsible AI use, Madala programmed Kora to flag when it is unsure, leaving the final judgment to the adult who intimately knows the child. He continues to lead the app's development with a team of three colleagues, aiming to expand its reach to more therapy centers, schools, and families.</p><p>Madala's efforts have garnered significant recognition. In December 2025, Kora secured first place in the Congressional App Challenge for New Jersey’s 10th Congressional District, triumphing over approximately 4,600 participants. The app was subsequently displayed in the US Capitol. Further accolades followed in 2026 when a Joint Legislative Resolution from the New Jersey Senate and General Assembly lauded Madala for his victory. He reflected on his journey, stating, &quot;The most useful thing I have ever built came out of paying close attention to one person.&quot;</p><p>Why it matters: This innovative, free technology empowers caregivers to better understand autistic children's emotional needs, fostering improved communication and support.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is thorough, well-organized, and fully utilizes the provided source text to construct a complete news narrative.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.goodnewsnetwork.org/15-yo-teaches-himself-machine-learning-to-build-app-that-interprets-autistic-communication/" rel="noopener">Good News Network</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
