     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-09 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Tree-Rich Landscapes Boost Cacao Yields via Biodiversity</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Research from the University of Vienna indicates that tropical cacao agroforestry can protect biodiversity while significantly increasing yields. By studying 28 organic farms in the Peruvian Amazon, researchers found that birds and bats provide natural pest control that supports productivity. The study suggests that managing shade-tree structure is key, as overly dense shade reduces light and yields, while a mosaic of tall and short trees supports habitat without production losses.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-09/tree-rich-landscapes-boost-cacao-yields-via-biodiversity.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Landscape management outside the farm is equally vital to productivity. Yields jumped from 265 kg per hectare in deforested areas to approximately 1,000 kg per hectare in landscapes featuring medium forest cover and additional tree crops.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief is concise, accurate, and includes key quantitative data from the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-tree-rich-landscapes-birds-cacao.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
