       <h1>Zimbabwe Makes Test History with Double 500-Run Innings in Sharjah</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#Test Match</span> <span>#Zimbabwe</span> <span>#Records</span></div>
       <p>Zimbabwe etched their name into Test cricket history at the Sharjah Cricket Stadium between November 5 and 9, 2026, by becoming the first team ever to surpass 500 runs in both innings of a single Test match. They posted a formidable 504 in their first innings and then declared their second at 523 for 5. This remarkable achievement, a feat no side had managed across more than a century of Test cricket, as reported by Crictracker, sets a new benchmark in the sport's record books.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-09/zimbabwe-makes-test-history-with-double-500-run-innings-in-s.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The high-scoring contest saw Afghanistan reply with 514 in their own first innings, resulting in a combined total of 1,541 runs across three completed innings with only 25 wickets falling. Despite the match ending in a draw, the historic nature of Zimbabwe's batting effort entirely overshadowed the result. The game produced six centuries in total, including one double hundred, with both sides contributing to a run-feast remembered as one of the most extraordinary scoring contests.</p><p>Key contributors for Zimbabwe included Brendan Taylor, named Player of the Match for his 131 in the first innings, well-supported by Brian Bennett's 128, which provided the backbone of their initial 504. In the second innings, Craig Ervine took centre stage with an unbeaten 206, a double century scored at the age of 41 in only his first Test appearance. This knock made Ervine the third-oldest man to register a double century in Test cricket. Wessly Madhevere also remained unbeaten on 100 in the second innings, marking his maiden Test century, with their contributions powering Zimbabwe to their second 500+ total.</p><p>For Afghanistan, Rahmat Shah, Sediqullah Atal, and Rahmanullah Zadran each scored centuries in the first innings, ensuring the match remained competitive. Zimbabwe's back-to-back totals of 504 and 523 for 5 now stand alone, confirming their place in Test cricket history. The aggregate of 1,541 runs across the three completed innings, with minimal wickets falling, further underlines the dominant batting display throughout the five days at Sharjah.</p><p>What to watch: Future Test performances by Zimbabwe's batting lineup.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft thoroughly incorporates all facts, statistics, and context provided in the source text, maintaining a professional journalistic tone.</p>
